 "story": "A quiet week of data kept the same picture: firing is low, hiring is slow, and prices are the worry. Initial jobless claims slipped to 197K (200K expected), while continuing claims edged up to 1.716M. ISM services eased to 54.9 from 55.4, a hair under the 55.0 consensus, but its prices-paid index jumped to 74.0. Households are feeling it: preliminary October sentiment fell to 46.3 (47.6 expected) as year-ahead inflation expectations rose to 4.7%, and August consumer credit grew just $8.3B against $15B expected, with revolving balances down $4.8B. The 30-yr mortgage rate climbed to 7.40%. Stocks shrugged it off: the S&P 500 is up 14.1% YTD, with the 10-yr at 5.22%.",
